<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Alta California</h1><p class="st-meta">1769 CE – 1848 CE · capital Monterey</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern frontier of New Spain and then Mexico, settled through Franciscan missions and presidios before passing to the United States in 1848.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fearing Russian and British advances on the Pacific coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portola-expedition">Portolá expedition</a> north in 1769. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junipero-serra">Junípero Serra</a> founded a mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a> that year, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterey-california">Monterey</a>, founded in 1770, later became the capital. Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> was formally separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> in 1804 as a province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Twenty-one missions along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-camino-real-california">El Camino Real</a> gathered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigenous-peoples-of-california">Native Californians</a> into labour communities, with devastating losses from disease and coercion. Presidios and a few pueblos such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-california">San Jose</a> held the Spanish presence. After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> won independence in 1821, the missions were secularised in the 1830s and their lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/californios">Californio</a> ranchers, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> hides and tallow were traded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> ships.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/californios">Californio</a> politics were turbulent, with revolts against governors sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/california-republic">California Republic</a> briefly appeared in 1846 and US forces occupied the province. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a> ceded it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1848; statehood followed in 1850 amid the Gold Rush.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/alta-california.jpg" alt="Alta California" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: previous version User:Ignaciogavira ; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1769 CE – 1848 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Monterey</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gaspar de Portolá, Juan Bautista Alvarado, Pío Pico</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Upper California, Nueva California</dd></dl></aside></div>

